{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Know that</b> our holy Torah is bound with the supernal wisdom, and all the wisdoms, all of them, are included in it; and on this account it is called תמימה (perfect). And it restores the soul to its root. And the commandments given to us at Mount Sinai are 613 (תרי״ג), as is said (Deuteronomy 33:4): ״תורה צוה לנו משה״ — commandments according to the numerical value of תור״ה we received from Moses, and two commandments we heard and understood from the mouth of the Almighty, and behold they are 613 (תרי״ג). And it is a well-known thing that all the commandments, all of them, are divine, and they are weighed in the scales of wisdom, built upon the foundations of knowledge, established upon the pillars of understanding; and there is not a single commandment that does not include within it one species from the species of wisdom. And these 613 commandments are 613 general principles, for the particulars of the commandments have no end or limit, and reason dictates this: for since the commandments are divine and all of them testify to the obligation of the existence of His divinity and to knowledge of Him from the perspective of His ways and actions, just as there is no limit attainable by us from the perspective of knowledge of His actions, so too there is no limit to the particulars of the commandments. And to this the King David, peace be upon him, intended in saying (Psalms 119:96): ״לכל תכלה ראיתי קץ רחבה מצותך מאד״ — he explained that every thing has an end, but the commandments have no end. If so, each and every commandment of the 613 commandments is a general principle, each and every one encompassing many commandments, and so all of them, without end. And these 613 commandments are divided into two categories: rational (מושכלות) and received (מקובלות). The rational commandments are those commandments about which reason itself testifies and by which it discerns that had they not been written, the person of understanding would have derived them through his reason — such as the commandment of honoring father and mother, and the prohibitions of murder, adultery, theft, false testimony, and robbery, and similar ones. The received commandments are those commandments that a person would never attain, nor find them through his reason at all, but he needs tradition (קבלה) for them — such as the commandments of the shofar, the sukkah, and the lulav, the commandments of levirate marriage (יבום) and halitzah, the commandments of the Sabbatical year (שמיטה) and the Jubilee, the sacrifices, and the prohibition of mixed species (כלאים), and similar ones. And both these and those are for the benefit of body and soul. In the rational ones there is bodily benefit for the maintenance of the world, like the laws of the nations and their conduct, and the settling of lands and cities; and in them there is benefit for the soul as well. And in the received ones there is benefit for the soul, for through them the soul is elevated and attains, through their fulfillment, the life of the World to Come; and in them there is bodily benefit as well." "<b>And in order</b> to clarify that all the commandments in the Torah possess benefit for both body and soul, Moses our teacher, peace be upon him, testified, saying (Deuteronomy 6:24–25): ״לטוב לנו כל הימים לחיותנו כהיום הזה״, and it is written: ״וצדקה תהיה לנו כי נשמור לעשות וגו׳ לפני ה' אלהינו״. He explained that the fulfillment of the commandments includes two benefits: the benefit of the body as a whole in this world, and the benefit of the soul in the World to Come. That which he mentioned in general — ״לטוב לנו כל הימים״ — and afterward he added a clarification of general and particular: ״לחיותנו כהיום הזה״ — this is the benefit of the body in this world; ״וצדקה תהיה לנו לפני ה' אלהינו״ — this is the benefit of the soul in the World to Come. And so King Solomon, peace be upon him, explained this very matter, saying (Proverbs 4:22): ״כי חיים הם למוצאיהם ולכל בשרו מרפא״ — from this you learn that in all the commandments there is benefit for body and soul." "<b>And if</b> you ask and say: from where do we know from Scripture that all the wisdoms are included in the Torah and are drawn forth and emerge from it? Behold, Solomon, peace be upon him, explained this within the hints of his words, namely his saying (Song of Songs 4:11): ״נופת תטופנה שפתותיך כלה דבש וחלב תחת לשונך וריח שלמותיך כריח לבנון״ — he compared the Torah to a bride (כלה), as our Rabbis of blessed memory drashed (Numbers 7:1): ״וביום כלת משה״ — ״כלת״ is written (without the vav), on the day she entered the bridal canopy; and our Rabbis of blessed memory further drashed: do not read ״מורשה״ (inheritance) but rather ״מאורשה״ (betrothed). And Solomon the King, peace be upon him, taught you in this verse that all the wisdoms, which are likened to honeycomb dripping (נופת), all of them drip from the lips of the bride. And the reason he likened them to honeycomb (נופת) is that all the wisdoms other than the wisdom of our Torah all have admixture and dross in them, like this honeycomb that includes honey and wax; but the wisdom of our holy Torah is pure honey, clean of wax and of all impurity. This is his saying (Song of Songs 4:11): ״דבש וחלב תחת לשונך״ — for honey and milk are clear things, clean without dross. And so King David, peace be upon him, likened our Torah to refined silver that has no dross, as he said (Psalms 12:7): ״אמרת ה' אמרות טהורות כסף צרוף וגו״. And since in the clean and pure wisdoms of our Torah there are matters that are pure and worthy of being concealed, on this account he said: ״תחת לשונך״ — that is, you are obligated to keep them hidden and not bring them forth from your mouth, in the manner of what is written (Proverbs 5:17): ״יהיו לך לבדך ואין לזרים אתך״. And so our Rabbis of blessed memory drashed: What is ״ולמכסה עתיק״ (Isaiah 23:18)? — matters that the Ancient of Days (עתיק יומין) has covered, do not reveal them. And he took as an example one commandment from the Torah that is among the matters worthy of being concealed, and it is the commandment of tzitzit; this is (Song of Songs 4:11): ״וריח שלמותיך כריח לבנון״. Now you already know that ריח (scent) is the remembrance (זכרון) of a thing, in the manner of what is written concerning the sacrifices (Leviticus 6:8): ״אזכרתה ריח ניחוח״, and it is written concerning tzitzit (Numbers 15:39): ״וראיתם אותו וזכרתם״. And he said that the remembrance of those commandments is like the remembrance of Lebanon. And Lebanon (הלבנון) is the wisdom to which the commandment of tzitzit alludes; and the 32 threads (ל״ב חוטין) correspond to the 32 wondrous paths of wisdom (ל״ב נתיבות פליאות חכמה). And understand this. Now our Rabbis of blessed memory already explained to us the excellence of the Torah and its perfection, and how it includes the supernal beings and the lower beings, to the point that they informed us that had the Torah been written in its proper order, a person would have been able to revive the dead with it. Thus they said in the Midrash (Psalms 3:1): ״מזמור לדוד בברחו מפני אבשלום בנו״ — what is meant by what is written ״לא ידע אנוש ערכה״ (Job 28:13)? Said R. Elazar: the sections of the Torah were not written in their order; had they been written in their order, whoever reads in them could have revived the dead and performed wonders. Therefore the order of the Torah was concealed, and even though its order was concealed, it is revealed before the Holy One, blessed be He, as is said (Isaiah 44:7): ״ומי כמוני יקרא ויגידה ויערכה לי משומי עם עולם ואותיות ואשר תבואנה יגידו למו״ — end of quotation. And now, since it was not written in its order, a person merits through it the life of the World to Come; as King Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Proverbs 3:16): ״ארך ימים בימינה בשמאלה עושר וכבוד״ — he called the World to Come ״right,״ for there a person merits length of days forever, since it is a world that is entirely long; and he called this world ״left,״ and mentioned regarding its excellence ״wealth and honor,״ for just as wealth and honor are a preparation for a person in this world for receiving the rational truths (מושכלות), so too this world is a preparation for acquiring through it the life of the World to Come. And Solomon the King, peace be upon him, taught you in this verse that the World to Come contains length of days forever, and this world is a preparation toward it. And it is a well-known thing that Moses our teacher, peace be upon him, the master of all the prophets — all that he knew and attained in the gates of understanding, the whole of it he knew and attained from the Torah during the forty days and forty nights he stood on the mountain. Also King Solomon — it is known and famous that his wisdom was broader than the sea, and Scripture testified about him (I Kings 5:11): ״ויחכם מכל האדם״, and there is no doubt that through the Torah he learned everything. And the masters of truth testified about his great and exalted excellence that he ascended to through the wisdom of the Torah, as they said: Solomon ruled over the supernal beings and over the lower beings, as is said (I Chronicles 29:23): ״וישב שלמה על כסא ה' למלך״." "<b>And you should be roused</b> to ask here: why did Scripture call Solomon's throne ״the throne of HaShem,״ and how could Scripture compare a profane throne to a holy throne?" "<b>But</b> the matter is that Solomon's throne is a likeness (דוגמא). And our Rabbis of blessed memory, the masters of truth, the knowers of hidden things, whose words are like torches, brought on this an awesomely rousing teaching whose fragrance is like fine oil poured forth — a teaching that illuminates the eyes of the intellect and flashes like lightning. It is one of those teachings that they scattered in their books, treasured and concealed in their Midrashim. And it is what they said: ״תני רבי חייא: מל״ך עולה תשעים — this teaches that the glory of the Holy One, blessed be He, does not dwell in less than ninety thousand myriads of angels. And King Solomon — ninety thousand thrones were before him; ninety thousand cathedrae of gold he had surrounding the throne, and there the sages and their students would sit, officers and nobles, priests and Levites; and seventy cathedrae opposite the throne, corresponding to the seventy Sanhedrin; and two cathedrae facing him, one for Gad the seer and one for Nathan the prophet; and one throne at his right for Bathsheba his mother״ — end of quotation. All these cathedrae — the ninety thousand and the seventy and the two and the one — the whole is a likeness (דוגמא). And the reason this teaching divided its language between קתדרא (cathedra) and כסא (throne), and did not refer to all of them by the name cathedra or all by the name throne, is all for an intended purpose. And so too what is written about Solomon's throne (I Kings 10:19–20): ״והידת מזה ומזה אל מקום השבת ושנים אריות עומדים אצל הידת״, and it is written ״ושנים עשר אריים עומדים שם על שש המעלת מזה ומזה לא נעשה כן לכל ממלכות״ — the arms (הידות) of the throne and the twelve lions, the whole is a likeness (דוגמא). The arms are the attributes (מדות) through which the world was created, in the manner of what is written (Isaiah 48:13): ״אף ידי יסדה ארץ וימיני טפחה שמים״. And the two lions are the two mighty princes known by their names, one on the right and one on the left — and this is what it says: ״אצל הידות.״ And the twelve lions are twelve chieftains surrounding the throne, three for each direction of the four directions of the world, for so was the arrangement of the standards (דגלים) in the wilderness, that they surrounded the Ark. And on this account it was fitting for Solomon's throne to be called ״the throne of HaShem.״ And from where did Solomon know the arrangements of the exalted and lofty One, and the awesomeness of the mighty sovereignty and the wondrous and supreme elevation that is in the Throne of Glory, so that he made in likeness of it a throne ״that was not so made for all the kingdoms״? All this he knew and attained through the wisdom of the Torah. And from where did sovereignty come to him to rule over the supernal beings, if not through the wisdom of the Torah, which is from the supernal beings? And we find that through his great wisdom that he learned from the Torah, he composed three books, and they are Proverbs, Song of Songs, and Ecclesiastes. And it is his saying (I Kings 5:11): ״ויחכם מכל האדם מאיתן האזרחי והימן וכלכל ודרדע בני מחול״, and it is written (I Kings, same place): ״וידבר שלשת אלפים משל ויהי שירו חמשה ואלף״, and it is written (I Kings, same place): ״וידבר על העצים מן הארז אשר בלבנון ועד האזוב אשר יצא בקיר וידבר על הבהמה ועל העוף ועל הרמש ועל הדגים.״ These verses explain the abundance of wisdom that belonged to Solomon, which he encompassed in these three compositions. And this is what it says: ״וידבר שלשת אלפים משל״." "<b>And it appears to me</b> that 'eleph' is from the language of learning wisdom, in the manner of what is written ״ואאלפך חכמה״ (Job 33:33). And 'three thousand' (שלשת אלפים) is as if he said 'three compositions,' and he alluded to them by their names: the first, Mishlei; and the second, Shir HaShirim — this is what he said, ״ויהי שירו״ alluding to Shir HaShirim. And he mentioned ״חמשה ואלף״ — literally the letter אל״ף — and the intention is that the foundation of Shir HaShirim is joined upon the foundation of the final ה׳ of the Name הוי״ה, which is called 'all' (כל); therefore he always calls it ״אחותי כלה״, for the word כלה embraces both of them: the word כל and the letter ה׳ at the end. And when he said ״ויהי שירו חמשה ואלף״ he explained that the foundation of his song and its root was the letter ה׳. And ״וידבר על העצים״ — 'and he spoke about the trees' — from the cedar that is in Lebanon, this is the book of Kohelet, which is founded upon the wisdom of nature. Observe how he used the language ״וידבר״ in all the compositions, but in the composition of Shir HaShirim, which is holy of holies, he used in it the language of הויה and said ״ויהי שירו״, because it is joined upon the foundation of the הויה." "<b>And among the greatnesses</b> of the Torah is that it was created before the world was created, and so our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: the Torah was written before Him in black fire upon white fire. And this is one of the intentions in בראשית — that the Torah began with the letter בי״ת, meaning two thousand (שני אלפים), which is ראשית. And were it not so, and had Moses our master, peace be upon him, written merely as a copyist of an earlier book, it would have been fitting for him to say at the beginning of the Torah: 'And Elohim spoke all these words to Moses.' Therefore he began with what he began. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: Rabbi Yitzchak said, the Torah did not need to begin with anything but ״בראשית ברא אלהים״ — rather it should have begun with ״החדש הזה לכם״ (Exodus 12:2), because that is the first commandment with which Israel was commanded. And why did he open with בראשית? Because of (Psalms 111:6) ״כח מעשיו הגיד לעמו״. And the intention is to say that since the commandments are the essence of the Torah, it was not fitting for the opening of the words of the Torah to be anything but a commandment. And what business do we have recounting the creation of the world? It could have been that the matter would be handed over to the tradition of Israel and not written in the book of the Torah. But the answer was: ״כח מעשיו הגיד לעמו״ — that is, He drew down for us the power of His deeds, for His kingship was made known from the side of His ways and His actions in actuality — that He created and brought into being from nothingness all existent beings. The Holy One, blessed be He, did not wish this great matter to be left in the hands of tradition alone, but rather that the Torah itself testify to it; for through belief in the creation of the world, a person attains knowledge of HaShem from the side of His ways and His actions. And this is the particular apprehension that is possible, for apprehension from the side of His essence and His nature is impossible, since His matter is hidden and concealed even from the angels. And this great matter is included in one verse from the book of Mishlei, namely: ״כבוד אלהים הסתר דבר וכבוד מלכים חקור דבר״ (Proverbs 25:2). King Solomon, peace be upon him, revealed to us in this verse that knowledge of HaShem is of two parts: impossible and possible. The impossible is investigation into apprehending Him from the side of His essence and His nature, and concerning this he said ״הסתר דבר״. The possible is investigation of His kingship from the side of His ways and His actions, and concerning this he said ״וכבוד מלכים חקור דבר״. And this verse is joined with the verse after it (ibid.): ״שמים לרום וארץ לעומק״ — which are the totality of existence. And he informed us through this that this possible investigation is ״שמים לרום וארץ לעומק״ — which is the totality of all existence. For the word שמים includes the spheres, and the word לרום includes the angels, which are above them; and ארץ לעומק includes all created beings of the lower world, dry land and sea, to the depths of the abysses. And ״ולב מלכים אין חקר״ corresponds to the first part, the impossible, concerning which he mentioned ״הסתר דבר״. And he mentioned ״לב מלכים״ because the essence of the Name that is hidden — there is no investigating it, for this is among the impossible. And in the manner of what is said to Iyov (Job 11:7): ״החקר אלוה תמצא אם עד תכלית שדי תמצא״." "<b>And behold,</b> Moses our master, peace be upon him, asked these two things (Exodus 33:18–19, 23): ״הודיעני נא את דרכך״ — this is the possible part, and he was answered in this: ״אני אעביר כל טובי על פניך״; ״הראני נא את כבודך״ — this is the impossible part, and he was answered in this: ״לא תוכל לראות את פני״. And behold, these two — just as a person is obligated and commanded to refrain from investigating the impossible part, so too is he commanded and obligated to seek and investigate the possible part, for through this a servant finds favor with his master, and we know Him through His actions. Therefore wisdom required that the Torah begin with the creation of the world, to declare to His people the power of His deeds. And since all the roots of faith lean upon and depend upon belief in creation, for creation is a proof of providence, and providence is a proof of the greatness of prophecy and of the truth of reward and punishment — and all these great principles are elucidated from this passage — He first informed us that the world is newly created, brought into being from nothingness, after its beginning had been תהו ובהו; He created all existent beings in six days, and on the sixth He created man, and exercised providence over him in three respects: in creation (בריאה), in formation (יצירה), and in making (עשיה). And He commanded him with positive commandments and negative commandments, and all this is testimony and absolute proof of providence and of prophecy — for YHWH spoke with man and commanded him to do what He desires and to refrain from what He distances from him. And along with this, reward and punishment for the righteous and the wicked were elucidated: reward was elucidated in that He placed him in the Garden of Eden when he was worthy, as it says (Genesis 2:15) ״ויניחהו בגן עדן״; and punishment was elucidated in that when he sinned He banished him from there, as it says (Genesis 3:24) ״ויגרש את האדם״. And likewise the punishment of Cain for murder was elucidated, and the reward of Enoch, who walked with the Elohim; and likewise the punishment of the men of the generation of the Flood for the act of harlotry, and that is what brought the Flood upon them; and the reward of Noah the righteous, who found favor in the eyes of YHWH and was saved. You have thus learned that all these mighty principles follow from belief in the creation of the world, and the entire Torah in its entirety is founded and built upon them — and each one necessitates the next: creation of the world necessitates providence, and providence necessitates reward and punishment, and necessitates prophecy, and from within prophecy comes the Torah. If so, it was necessary for the Torah to begin with the creation of the world, and this is what is written: <b>In the beginning Elohim created the heavens and the earth.</b>" "<b>בראשית.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): the word בראשית is as if it said 'at first' (בראשונה); and the reason it did not say בראשונה is that the word בראשית embraces great and mighty meanings, many and weighty, which the word בראשונה would not embrace." "<b>And according to the opinion of Rashi</b> the word ראשית is in construct (סמוכה), and so too every instance of בראשית in Scripture; and nothing was created on the first day except light alone. And the construal of the verse is: 'At the beginning of the creation of the heavens and the earth — and the earth was unformed and void — Elohim said, Let there be light,' and everything follows after the light. And he said that it is like (Hosea 1:2): ״תחלת דבר ה׳ בהושע ויאמר ה׳ אל הושע״. And his opinion, of blessed memory, is that if the intention of the verse had been that the heavens and the earth were created on the first day, it should have written בראשונה." "<b>But</b> the principal point is that it is a word standing on its own, not construct-bound, and it is as though it said 'at first' (בראשונה). The proof of this is the reason of the word itself, and the sense of the section: that the heavens and the earth with all their derivatives were created on the first day from absolute nothingness and utter nonexistence. And so too the language of ברא testifies, which is the language of bringing forth something from nothing. This is the view of R. Neḥemiah, that on the first day the entire world was created; as they said in the Midrash: R. Yehudah and R. Neḥemiah — R. Yehudah says: in six days the entire world was created, for thus is written regarding the work of each and every day, ״ויהי כן״; R. Neḥemiah says: on the first day the entire world was created, for it is written, תוצא הארץ — a thing that is ordered and prepared in its hand from בראשית. To what may the matter be compared? To a man who sowed six barley grains at once — this one sprouted on the first day, and this one on the second, and this one on the third, and so all of them. And the meaning of R. Neḥemiah in this parable is clear: the matter from which everything was made was created on the first day, and afterward all the created beings were brought forth during the six days from the portions of that matter, each one on its day. And this is what is written (Psalms 33): ״כי הוא אמר ויהי״ — it explains that everything was in a single utterance and a single coming-into-being, and afterward He commanded each thing on its day and established the things and brought them forth into actuality." "<b>Elohim.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): a term of divinity, and its meaning is the possessor of all powers. It is for this reason that it did not say El or Eloah, but rather Elohim, in the plural form. And so too (Joshua 24:19): ״אלהים קדושים הוא״ — for He encompasses all holinesses. And so too the manner of language regarding the rule of earthly potentates: אדונים קשה — in the plural form — and this is on account of the greatness of the lord's preeminence and the multiplicity of the kinds of dominion he possesses. And this is so regarding flesh and blood — how much more so regarding HaShem, blessed be He, whose kinds of dominion have no number, and all rulers have no dominion except from Him. And He is one, and His powers are many. And so too the custom of language in other words (Exodus 12:42): ״ליל שמורים הוא״; ״יום כפורים הוא״. Furthermore, one may explain that He is called Elohim in the creation of the world because He created His world with wisdom and understanding, as it is written (Proverbs 3:19): ״ה' בחכמה יסד ארץ כונן שמים בתבונה״." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> This name means 'judge.' And this is what is written (Exodus 22:27): ״אלהים לא תקלל״, and Onkelos translated it: דיינא. And a full verse is (Psalms 75:8): ״כי אלהים שופט זה ישפיל וזה ירים״. And He is called here by this name on account of His having created the world with the attribute of judgment." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah:</b> This name is two words: אל–הם, and they are the explanation of the yod in (Ecclesiastes 12:1): ״וזכר את בוראיך״ — spelled full with a yod. And whoever understands will understand." "<b>Et the heavens and et the earth.</b> The word את in most cases comes to include. By way of the plain sense (peshat), these two instances of את come to include the two matters that the Holy One, blessed be He, created: one from which He made the heavens, and one from which He made the earth. And this is what it says: ״והארץ היתה תהו ובהו״ — it explains that the earth, after this act of creation, was תהו ובהו. And the meaning of תהו ובהו is matter and form — that is to say, He clothed the matter with form. And this is (Isaiah 66:2): ״ואת כל אלה ידי עשתה ויהיו כל אלה״. It says ואת כל אלה — that is, all these existing things, ״ידי עשתה״, my hand made; and afterward, ״ויהיו כל אלה״ — and all these came to be in their present form. According to this, the matter was created first, and after it there was no further creation but only formation and making, for He brought forth everything from it and clothed the forms upon it. And in this act of creation, which was like a tiny, fine point having no substance, all the created beings in the heavens and on the earth were created." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: את השמים — 'the heavens' — to include the sun and the moon, the stars and the constellations; ואת הארץ — 'and the earth' — to include trees and grasses and the Garden of Eden. And all these things encompass every created being that possesses a body."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :register {:flags {0 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T23:21:52.500185089Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}